c.update(why='The 5 November Finance Ministry release advances the regular November tax-devolution instalment from the normal 10 November date. The complete primary narrative and state table were re-read.',facts=['The regular instalment is ₹1,09,019 crore; it was released on 5 November instead of 10 November in view of the festive season.','The primary table gives Andhra Pradesh ₹4,597 crore and Telangana ₹2,370 crore.','This is accelerated timing of a regular instalment, not an additional discretionary grant, a new sharing formula or proof that states spent the funds immediately.'],static_link='Articles 270 and 280 concern tax sharing and the Finance Commission framework. Vertical sharing, state-wise horizontal distribution, grants and the timing of a release are different questions.',analysis=['Earlier cash availability can help states manage welfare payments and capital works without changing the full-year entitlement. Evaluate predictability, cash management and actual execution rather than interpreting every release as extra fiscal space.','For APPSC, retain the AP/Telangana figures and distinguish union release, state treasury receipt, departmental allotment and final expenditure. This note retains CA-043 to preserve bookmarks and earlier references.'],evidence_access='Complete unique primary release and state table read in opening chunk 0/3 on 7 November. No separate treasury settlement or final expenditure records inspected.',checked='2026-11-07',status='Expanded historical first-week note; regular instalment and release date primary-read. Daily-source audit remains incomplete.')
A['NOV'].update(access=c['evidence_access'],checked_on='2026-11-07');S['NOV'][1]=pib(2333166)
def add(subject,block,title,why,facts,static,analysis,trap,mains,src,day,p='P1',ap=False,report=False):
 J.append(dict(id=f'E18-2611-{len(J)+1:03}',date=f'2026-11-{day:02}',date_precision='reporting-day'if report else 'day',subject=subject,block=block,title=title,why=why,facts=facts.split('|'),static_link=static,analysis=analysis.split('|'),trap=trap,mains=mains,priority=p,ap=ap,sources=['E18-'+k for k in src.split(',')],status='Selected 1–7 November research, not a full November month or completed daily audit. Announcements, targets, future effect and analytical applications are distinguished.',checked='2026-11-07',evidence_access='Actual reading depth is recorded in data/source-access.json and research/edition18/Research-Log.md. Static links, analysis, cautions and Mains prompts are original study guidance.'))
add('Economy','01 · Official statistics and evidence governance','VISTAR: a stronger statistical system needs trustworthy methods, not just more data','The 4 November Cabinet approves a central-sector statistical transformation scheme for 2026–27 to 2030–31. The primary release, rather than rounded coaching totals, anchors this note.','Outlay is ₹4,371.10 crore, with components for statistical operations/ecosystem, state statistical strengthening and the Economic Census.|MoSPI proposes 59 surveys in the 16th Finance Commission cycle and plans new service/unincorporated-enterprise indices; proposals are not already published series.|SSS 2.0 envisages deliverable-linked state capacity and granular estimates; the 8th Economic Census would cover non-farm establishments and underpin a statistical business register.','The Economic Census of establishments is distinct from the population Census. A business register, sample survey, national-account estimate and administrative record have different purposes.','Digital interoperability can reduce duplication, but representativeness, confidentiality, stable definitions and transparent revisions remain essential. Scanner/mobile/e-commerce data can complement rather than automatically replace careful surveys.|For AP, invest in district estimates, trained personnel and auditable methods. A scheme approval or AI-ready dataset is not proof that every district now has timely, reliable GDP or employment estimates.','The 8th Economic Census is not the eighth population Census; 59 planned surveys are not 59 completed results.','How can statistical modernisation improve policy targeting without weakening credibility and privacy?','VISTAR',4)
add('Polity & Governance','01 · Justice delivery and accountable institutions','Fast Track Special Courts: expand capacity without sacrificing fair process','The 4 November Cabinet continues the centrally sponsored scheme through 31 March 2031. The release distinguishes current functional courts from proposed capacity.','Outlay is ₹3,788.04 crore: central ₹2,353.15 crore and state/UT ₹1,434.89 crore; the central share comes from the Nirbhaya Fund.|Expansion is envisaged from 779 existing functional FTSCs to 850, including exclusive POCSO courts; additional districts depend on pendency.|Each court is provided for a dedicated judicial officer, special public prosecutor and six support staff; vulnerable-witness and child-friendly facilities are encouraged.','Fast-track designation, creation of posts, functional courtrooms and disposal quality are distinct. POCSO protects children; every FTSC is not necessarily an exclusive POCSO court.','Speed requires investigation, forensic capacity, prosecution, legal aid, witness support and reasoned decisions as well as judges. Disposal totals alone cannot establish victim wellbeing, fair trial or absence of reversals.|For AP, map case load and vacancies before assuming uniform additional courts in every district. Funding approval does not certify filled posts or permit dilution of evidentiary rights to achieve numerical targets.','850 is the proposed scheme capacity, not the verified existing functional total of 779.','How can special courts provide timely, child-sensitive justice while protecting due process?','FTSC',4)
add('Economy','02 · Industrial capability and strategic inputs','Critical-mineral processing: support refining capability, not merely mine allocation','The 4 November approval targets domestic high-purity lithium/nickel processing, a different stage from October’s mineral-auction changes.','The five-year 2026–31 outlay is ₹2,814 crore for two lithium plants of at least 30 kt each and two nickel plants of at least 50 kt each.|Capital subsidy is 30% of eligible investment, capped at ₹500 crore per lithium beneficiary and ₹900 crore per nickel beneficiary.|Technical qualification precedes least-cost support bidding; incentives have three milestone-linked tranches. Cobalt may be recovered as a nickel by-product, not a separately described fifth plant.','Mining, beneficiation, refining, precursor production, cell manufacture and recycling are different links. Domestic refining may still depend on imported feedstock, technology or equipment.','Assess raw-material security, recovery quality, waste/water management, energy and long-term offtake. Least subsidy sought is useful only alongside credible technical delivery and lifecycle safeguards.|For AP industrial analysis, connect battery/electronics demand without inventing a locally selected beneficiary. A subsidy ceiling is not guaranteed payment, and the ₹2,814 crore headline is not identical to aggregate private investment.','Two lithium plus two nickel plants do not mean four lithium plants or an automatically operational domestic supply chain.','How should critical-mineral policy balance processing capability, environmental safeguards and supply security?','MIN',4)
add('Economy','03 · Flexible power systems and public finance','Storage VGF: 92 GWh is supported future energy capacity, not power already available','The 4 November Cabinet approves ₹14,500 crore financial support for grid-scale storage during 2026–31. The release explicitly describes yet-to-be-commissioned capacity.','Target mix is 60 GWh pumped storage, 30 GWh BESS and 2 GWh alternative technologies, totalling 92 GWh.|Estimated total investment is ₹97,600 crore, distinct from the ₹14,500 crore VGF support.|Allocation uses tariff-based competitive bidding; BESS projects have a minimum 40% domestic-content requirement. Detailed awards/guidelines were not independently read.','GW is power, GWh is energy; duration depends on both. Storage shifts electricity with losses and is not a free new primary energy source. VGF differs from total project cost.','Choose services against system needs, renewable profiles and actual dispatchability. Contracts should address degradation, availability, safety, recycling, water and tariffs across technology-specific lifecycles.|For AP DISCOMs, national eligibility is not an automatic allocation or proof that storage assets are commissioned. Procurement and domestic-content verification must be measured alongside useful flexibility delivered.','92 GWh is not 92 GW, and approval does not mean the capacity is already operating.','How can storage support integrate renewables while protecting consumers and public finances?','ESS',4)
add('Polity & Governance','01 · Justice delivery and accountable institutions','PAC resolution: audit follow-up requires action, not a digital portal alone','The 3–4 November chairpersons’ conference adopts a resolution on public accounts oversight. Its text is a collective commitment, not a newly enacted uniform rule for all legislatures.','The resolution seeks standardised action-taken timelines and digital audit-para oversight in state/UT PACs.|It calls for training, analytical tools, peer learning and cooperation with the CAG.|It proposes considering at least three sittings monthly and subcommittees to address pendency; this is not independent proof that every state PAC already complies.','CAG audit and legislative PAC examination are complementary, not the same institution. An audit observation, committee recommendation, executive response and settled corrective action are separate stages.','Digital tracking can identify delayed replies, but reasoned scrutiny, documentary evidence and executive accountability determine results. Protect due process and avoid turning a portal status into automatic proof of wrongdoing or exoneration.|For APPSC, connect expenditure authorisation, intended purpose and follow-up rather than equating regular meetings with effective oversight. Compare reduced recurrent irregularities and substantive action, not only uploaded reports.','A conference resolution does not automatically amend every legislature’s rules or transfer CAG’s constitutional role to the PAC.','How can legislative financial oversight close the gap between audit findings and administrative correction?','PAC',4)
add('Polity & Governance','02 · Digital services and consumer rights','Network Anubhav: interpret user-test maps with sampling and percentile caution','TRAI launches a geospatial network-experience dashboard on 4 November, combining consumer observations and independent drive-test information.','The platform draws on MySpeed/MyCall observations and shows provider, technology, area and speed information, with a DND web interface.|The release describes P80 mobile and P90 fixed-broadband values as speeds met or exceeded by 80%/90% of tests; preserve that exceedance convention rather than silently imposing an ordinary lower-tail percentile definition.|TRAI explicitly says user-test values are not a comprehensive assessment under standard network-quality methodology.','Crowdsourced tests are not a representative census of subscribers. Median, exceedance threshold, average and guaranteed service speed are different measures.','Self-selection, handset, location, time and connectivity affect observations. Use maps to locate problems and guide independent checks, not to promise each household the displayed performance.|For AP, compare rural/coastal and urban data density and accessibility. More measurements improve coverage only when interpretation and privacy safeguards are credible; a dashboard is not an automatic remedy or compensation determination.','P80 in this release means 80% of tests at or above the stated speed; the source’s convention and sampling limits matter.','How can consumer-generated telecom data improve accountability without misleading users?','NET',4,p='P2')
add('Polity & Governance','02 · Digital services and consumer rights','Telecom grievance reform: 24-hour support is not 24 hours of human staffing','The 2 November release announces final complaint-redressal amendments after consultation. The complete regulation and commencement clause were not separately inspected.','The release says ISP applicability uses more than 10,000 subscribers at the preceding financial year-end, removing the alternative ₹5 crore turnover criterion.|Support becomes round-the-clock, with human help at least 16 hours daily and supervised technology allowed for other support; accessible channels for persons with disabilities are emphasised.|Appeal filing after complaint disposal changes from 30 to 15 days; the appellate authority’s decision within 15 days of receipt is a separate timeline. Quarterly feedback/board reporting and oversight mechanisms are described.','Provider complaint handling, internal appeal and regulatory review differ. A final regulation announcement is stronger than a consultation, but operational dates/exceptions still require the legal instrument.','Multiple channels and accessible help matter when automated systems misunderstand language or repeat generic answers. Shorter filing windows also require clear notification and assistance, not an assumption that speed always helps vulnerable consumers.|For AP, assess resolution quality, wrongful closures and accessibility alongside complaint counts. Consumer organisations and audits should scrutinise whether reported satisfaction reflects actual participation.','Round-the-clock support does not require the release’s minimum human staffing to be 24 hours; the stated minimum is 16.','How should telecom grievance reform combine convenience, inclusion and meaningful remedies?','RED',2)
add('Society & Welfare','01 · Service access and health affordability','International Digi Yatra trials: credentials complement, not replace, border formalities','A 1 November release starts November–December trials for eligible Indian e-passport holders on selected Air India/IndiGo international services.','Seven trial airports are Delhi, Bengaluru, Hyderabad, Cochin, Mumbai, Chennai and Amritsar; AP airports are not automatically included.|Credential creation uses the passport MRZ, embedded chip via NFC, a compatible phone and selfie verification, stored in the device wallet.|Mandatory travel documents, airline checks and immigration continue; feedback informs a proposed wider 2027 rollout.','An e-passport, identity credential, visa, boarding authorisation and permission to enter another country are different. NFC is short-range communication, not satellite navigation.','Convenience must be supported by clear consent, data minimisation, accessible alternatives and reliable correction when identity matching fails. Device-local storage is one design feature, not independent proof the entire ecosystem is risk-free.|For AP travellers, verify airport/flight eligibility and retain documentation. A pilot should be evaluated through failure rates, inclusion and privacy, not advertised as mandatory universal international travel clearance.','A Digi Yatra credential does not replace a passport, visa requirement or immigration check.','How should digital identity improve travel without creating exclusion or privacy risks?','DIGI',1,p='P2')
add('Agriculture','01 · Animal-health surveillance and rural livelihoods','VAJRA-SAT: preparedness for SAT-1 is not an Indian outbreak announcement','DAHD and ICAR-NIVEDI conduct a national tabletop exercise on 4–5 November; the 6 November release explicitly says FMD SAT-1 has not been reported in India.','Simulated scenarios address early detection, laboratory confirmation, movement control, epidemiology, vaccine readiness and coordinated communication.|Participants include state departments, laboratories, research institutions, vaccine manufacturers and border/security agencies.|A separate discussion concerns FMD-free compartmentalisation of semen centres/banks; a technical meeting does not certify every establishment disease-free.','Foot-and-mouth disease affects livestock and differs from human hand-foot-and-mouth disease. Serotype-specific surveillance and laboratory confirmation matter; an exercise is not a confirmed disease event.','Preparedness requires trained field staff, specimen transport, trusted reporting and clear escalation. Farmers need credible guidance and livelihood support rather than panic caused by misreading a simulation.|For AP livestock systems, connect October animal-reporting reforms with operational readiness. Neither participation numbers nor a tabletop scenario prove universal vaccine compatibility, freedom from every FMD serotype or completed outbreak control.','The inspected release states SAT-1 has not been reported in India; do not convert a simulation into a domestic outbreak.','How can veterinary preparedness protect livelihoods while preventing misinformation about disease risks?','FMD',5)
add('Environment','01 · Landscape governance and ecological recovery','SUVIKALP: a mine-repurposing ranking is not an environmental clearance','The 3 November Coal Ministry release explains CCO’s mine-repurposing decision-support platform, aligned with 2025 planning guidelines.','SUVIKALP combines site characteristics, environment, infrastructure, resource constraints, finance and socioeconomic factors.|It uses multi-criteria TOPSIS ranking; RECLAIM integrates community priorities and PRISM guides implementation pathways.|The described next phase includes feasibility studies, DPRs, funding, wider adoption and outcome monitoring; recommendations are not completed restoration projects.','Mine closure, rehabilitation, repurposing, environmental permission and title/land-use decisions are distinct. TOPSIS ranks alternatives relative to defined criteria and weights, not universal objective truth.','Transparent assumptions and meaningful participation are necessary because weighting choices can favour certain uses or actors. Test safety, contamination, water, long-term maintenance and local livelihoods before promoting new land use.|For AP mining landscapes, use this as a governance linkage rather than proof of a newly sanctioned local project. A tourism or solar recommendation cannot itself establish safe land, free consent or legal permission.','A high digital suitability rank is not a statutory clearance, financial closure or evidence of completed remediation.','How should post-mining development combine scientific assessment, community priorities and accountable implementation?','SUV',3,p='P2')
add('Security','01 · Personnel policy and capability development','Agniveer pathway: retain willingness and selection conditions','The 4 November Cabinet approval adds an engagement pathway for those outside the initial merit-based regular intake; detailed service orders were not independently read.','The earlier merit-based offer of regular employment for 25% is retained in the release.|Remaining 75% are offered another two-year engagement based on willingness.|After that period, regular enrolment is offered subject to willingness and meeting selection criteria; higher remuneration, enhanced Seva Nidhi and seniority benefits are announced without detailed rates here.','A policy pathway, an offer, acceptance, eligibility, selection and actual regular appointment differ. Temporary engagement and regular service have distinct terms.','Assess operational readiness, training continuity, predictable careers and fiscal sustainability together. Claims of performance in a government release are not a substitute for independent long-term evaluation.|For AP aspirants, rely on official recruitment/service instructions for individual decisions. Do not promise unconditional absorption on the announcement date or invent precise pay/seniority calculations absent the operative orders.','The pathway is conditional; it is not automatic immediate regular appointment of every individual regardless of willingness or selection.','How can military personnel policy balance readiness, skills, fair careers and sustainable costs?','AGN',4)
add('Security','01 · Personnel policy and capability development','Next-generation corvettes: signed contracts are beyond AoN, but not delivery','On 2 November the Ministry signs ₹53,866.50 crore contracts for eight corvettes. This is a later procurement stage than October’s separate in-principle AoN proposals.','GRSE is to construct five ships and Goa Shipyard three; the Navy provides indigenous design under the described project.|Intended roles span surface/sub-surface warfare, coastal defence, surveillance and protection of maritime routes.|Expected employment is expressed as 72 lakh shipyard and 460 lakh ancillary person-days over ten years, not those numbers of permanent workers.','A corvette, destroyer, submarine and aircraft carrier are different platforms. Design, contract, construction, trials, delivery and commissioning remain separate stages.','Evaluate build quality, integration, maintenance, supply resilience and delivery schedules. Indigenous design strengthens capability but does not prove every component is domestic or all future costs are fixed.|For AP’s maritime-industrial ecosystem, analyse supplier opportunities without assigning these specific shipbuilding contracts to Visakhapatnam. Employment estimates are prospective and cannot be treated as realised jobs.','Five plus three ships are contracted, not eight already commissioned ships or 532 lakh permanent jobs.','How should naval procurement be assessed across contracts, domestic capability and lifecycle readiness?','COR',2)
add('Security','01 · Personnel policy and capability development','Pralay test: user evaluation is not confirmed operational induction','DRDO reports a successful quasi-ballistic missile test off Odisha on 5 November, with test objectives met according to range data.','The test takes place from Dr APJ Abdul Kalam Island, with instrumentation from Integrated Test Range, Chandipur.|Research Centre Imarat, Hyderabad, and other DRDO laboratories developed the system; BDL is a development-cum-production partner.|The release describes user evaluation and says induction is imminent, not already completed. Exact range/warhead claims are not adopted without the appropriate source.','Quasi-ballistic trajectories differ from a simple ideal ballistic path; the label is not synonymous with a cruise missile. Development, production, evaluation and deployment have different meanings.','Operational capability needs reliability, training, logistics, command arrangements and integration beyond a successful test. Do not infer universal performance under every condition from one release.|For APPSC, distinguish Telangana’s Hyderabad institution from the Odisha test location and avoid a fabricated AP launch site. Link indigenous research with production while preserving the narrow scope of observed test success.','“Induction imminent” is not independently confirmed operational induction or combat deployment.','Why should military technology evaluation distinguish development milestones from usable operational capability?','PRAL',5,p='P2')
add('Environment','01 · Landscape governance and ecological recovery','I-OECMs: conserve beyond protected areas without changing ownership','Guidelines were released at CBD COP-17 in Yerevan on 3 November and reported on 4 November. The release names the Srisailam–Cumbum AP/Telangana demonstration landscape.','Recognition concerns defined areas outside already protected/conserved coverage that deliver sustained in-situ biodiversity outcomes; it does not itself create a protected area or alter ownership/legal status.|The release describes NBA preliminary review, state biodiversity validation, interdepartmental decision and measurable monitoring at least every five years.|The supporting GEF-8 project spans 2026–2032 and six demonstration landscapes/seascapes; landscape inclusion is not blanket recognition of every local parcel.','An OECM is not synonymous with a national park, sanctuary or UNESCO label. Conserved outcomes, governance, reporting and property rights are separate considerations.','Safeguard local institutions, existing rights and credible ecological monitoring. Avoid double counting already reported protected areas or treating recognition alone as proof of lasting biodiversity gains.|For APPSC, connect tanks, sacred groves, coastal ecosystems and the named landscape with local biodiversity management. Exact eligibility and rights implications require the full guidelines; no automatic new land restriction or tourist permission is inferred here.','OECM recognition does not automatically transfer ownership or turn the whole Srisailam–Cumbum landscape into a new national park.','How can area-based conservation expand while respecting local governance and credible ecological outcomes?','OECM',3,ap=True)
add('Society & Welfare','01 · Service access and health affordability','Anti-cancer price revision: the 7 November release points to 9 November effect','NPPA’s 7 November update reports revised prices under an October trade-margin order, with effect from 9 November—after this archive’s research cut-off.','The underlying intervention identifies 137 drugs; companies have reported revised MRPs for 1,225 formulations covering 121 drugs, with reductions in 1,031 formulations.|Maximum permissible trade margin is 30% under the described order; this is not an identical 30% price cut on every product.|₹3,502.31 crore annual consumer savings are estimated, not audited realised savings. The original NPPA notification was not independently inspected.','A drug molecule, formulation, brand, MRP, trade margin and total treatment cost are different denominators. Price regulation does not change an individual’s clinical prescription.','Monitor availability, compliance, displayed prices and complaints alongside affordability; diagnostic, hospital and travel costs can remain high. Reduced list prices are not proof every patient can access the required care.|For AP, strengthen price-check information and state drug-control follow-up without claiming the future 9 November prices were already universally operative on 7 November. Brand tables are not treatment advice or automatic interchangeability guidance.','137 drugs do not equal 1,225 distinct molecules; 30% margin is not a uniform 30% reduction or an already observed future outcome.','How should medicine-price regulation improve affordability without overlooking availability and total care costs?','CANCER',7)
add('Economy','02 · Industrial capability and strategic inputs','Semicon portal and ECMS: two programmes, different approval denominators','The 6 November release opens Semicon 2.0 portal applications for pillars 2, 3 and 4 and separately announces 12 additional ECMS approvals. Only the opening substantive portion, not every cumulative tail, was read.','Semicon portal coverage concerns machines/materials, more fabs and ATMP/OSAT; six strategic pillars are described across the wider programme.|The new 12 ECMS approvals project ₹7,503 crore investment, ₹39,045 crore production and 7,127 direct employment opportunities.|The listed new ECMS project states are Gujarat, Karnataka, Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, Punjab, Tamil Nadu and Uttar Pradesh; AP is not in that specific list.','Chip design, wafer fabrication, packaging/testing, electronic components and manufacturing equipment are different parts of the value chain. Application access is not project approval or commercial output.','Evaluate technology depth, skills, supplier quality, water/power reliability, yields and market demand. Avoid adding a programme’s fiscal outlay to projected private investment as though they were the same accounting measure.|For AP, explore ecosystem linkages without inventing a newly selected state project from this batch. Prospective production/jobs require later commissioning and audited outcomes; opening a portal does not establish a working fab.','The 12 ECMS approvals are not necessarily 12 new semiconductor fabs; projected jobs are not verified appointments.','How can electronics policy deepen domestic capabilities beyond assembly and headline investment approvals?','SEMI',6)
add('Science & Technology','01 · Foundation models and trustworthy deployment','IndiaAI models: local language and offline capability need independent evaluation','The 3 November MeitY release announces EKA-1 by Soket and Dhīr 2B/5B by ZenteiQ under the IndiaAI Innovation Centre pillar. Model cards, licences and performance were not independently tested.','The release describes EKA-1 as 26.3 billion parameters, trained on approximately 7.5 trillion tokens across 22 scheduled Indian languages and English.|Dhīr models are described as supporting English and ten Indian languages, with on-premise/offline-capable document-grounded use.|Parameter counts, training tokens, supported languages and benchmark performance measure different things; ministry/developer claims are not our independent validation.','A foundation model, retrieval system, application and agent are not identical. Offline deployment can limit external transfer but does not itself eliminate hallucination, insecure access or malicious inputs.','Test language/dialect performance, factual reliability, bias, latency and data handling for the actual task. Claims of openness require inspecting the exact licence, weights and restrictions, not relying on the label alone.|For AP service design, evaluate Telugu accessibility, human escalation and auditable decisions before consequential deployment. Public compute support and model release do not establish safe autonomous decisions about pensions, health or admissions.','A larger parameter count or on-device operation does not guarantee accuracy, privacy or approval for every public-sector use.','How should indigenous foundation-model development be linked to safe, inclusive public-service deployment?','AI',3)
add('International Relations','01 · Bay of Bengal cooperation and disaster readiness','BIMSTEC maritime exercise: event completion and release dates differ','A 6 November release reports the maiden exercise held 30 October–3 November under Andaman and Nicobar Command, concluding at Sri Vijaya Puram on 3 November.','BIMSTEC has seven members: Bangladesh, Bhutan, India, Myanmar, Nepal, Sri Lanka and Thailand.|The release separately lists participating Indian, Myanmar and Sri Lankan ships and representatives/delegations; membership is not evidence that all seven sent naval vessels.|The event combines harbour engagements and sea activity off Sri Vijaya Puram; it advances cooperation rather than creating a collective-defence treaty.','Landlocked membership is compatible with regional maritime cooperation. The Andaman and Nicobar Islands are strategically located near important Bay of Bengal/Indian Ocean routes.','Information sharing, communication, interoperability and sustained institutions matter beyond exercise publicity. Avoid treating a short activity as a resolution of all political differences or maritime crime.|For AP, connect sea-lane security,ports,fisheries and HADR coordination without assuming a new automatic regional military deployment commitment. Preserve the October start, November completion and later reporting date to avoid duplicated chronology.','A seven-member organisation does not imply seven navies each deployed ships; 6 November is the report date, not the exercise’s completion.','How can BIMSTEC translate maritime cooperation into practical regional public goods?','BIM',3,p='P2')
add('Andhra Pradesh','01 · Maritime cooperation and public assets','Tiger Triumph: Visakhapatnam hosts a HADR planning exercise, not an actual cyclone mission','The 6 November release reports the fifth India–US tri-service edition, conducted 31 October–5 November and closed at Visakhapatnam on 5 November.','Staff exercises,expert exchanges and a tabletop simulation focus on combined HADR planning,including amphibious operations.|Cyberspace and medical-operation discussions support interoperability;the source does not establish an actual joint deployment in the local cyclone response.|Tiger Triumph began in 2019; partner,edition number,event dates and reporting date should be kept distinct.','HADR is humanitarian assistance and disaster relief. A planning exercise,a field rescue operation and a treaty obligation are different.','Pre-agreed procedures can improve communications,medical coordination,logistics and safety when disasters occur. Evaluate lessons translated into workable protocols rather than assuming a closing ceremony proves operational readiness everywhere.|For APPSC,connect Visakhapatnam’s naval role with civilian disaster institutions and respect for host-state decision-making. Exercises do not replace local warning,evacuation,shelter and lifeline responsibilities or prove foreign forces undertook the Murasu response.','A tri-service HADR exercise is not proof of an actual combined combat or cyclone-rescue operation.','How can joint HADR exercises strengthen coastal disaster response while preserving clear civilian coordination?','TIGER',5,p='P2',ap=True)
add('Andhra Pradesh','01 · Maritime cooperation and public assets','RINL land auction: successful bids are not independently verified settled receipts','NLMC’s 4 November release reports the second auction phase conducted on 3 November in Visakhapatnam. It is land-asset monetisation,not a reported sale of the entire steel enterprise.','Phase II covers114parcels with₹218crore successful bids and about17.96%premium;reserve price is₹184.8crore in prose versus₹184.81crore in the table.|The two phases total264parcels and₹584.33crore bid value;Phase I’s12October event is not a new November auction.|A third round for remaining parcels is prospective and conditional;settlement,title transfer and deployment of proceeds were not independently inspected.','Reserve price,successful bid,receivable,realised cash and net proceeds are different. Monetising surplus land differs from disinvestment in the operating company.','Transparent title,survey,land-use conditions,competition and disclosure help protect public value. A premium over reserve indicates a comparison with the chosen benchmark,not automatic proof of the best possible social/economic use.|For APPSC,assess public purpose,local planning and the steel enterprise’s asset position without inventing operational shutdown or privatisation completion. Keep person-days,jobs,investment and land receipts in separate accounting categories.','₹218crore successful bids are not independently verified final cash receipt or proof that the whole Visakhapatnam Steel Plant was sold.','What safeguards should govern public-land monetisation in an industrial city?','RINL',3,ap=True)
add('Andhra Pradesh','02 · Murasu chronology and accountable recovery','Murasu: distinguish confirmed coastal crossing from preliminary loss snapshots','TNIE/PTI’s6Novemberreport confirms the cyclone crossed near Ongole on Tuesday,3November;earlier2Novemberreporting described the expected crossing. A final IMD observational report was not inspected.','The report locates crossing between Nellore and Narsapur,close to Ongole;forecast timing/surge should not be promoted into final observed wind or water levels.|Reported loss is preliminary,around₹1,919crore;the same report quotes₹1,918.5crore while another6Novemberaccount gives₹1,919.59crore.|These are evolving source snapshots,not independently reconciled audited estimates;the earlier₹4,600crore paragraph concerned Arnab and must not be assigned to Murasu.','Consecutive cyclones compound exposure and vulnerability. A landfall event,weather warning,state loss memorandum,central assessment and relief sanction have different evidentiary stages.','Retain timestamps and responsible attribution when figures change. Avoid summing overlapping snapshots or suggesting a requested amount is money already received.|For APPSC,connect hazard preparedness,evacuation and recovery while treating final fatalities,maximum winds and total losses cautiously. An early reported count is not a permanent final record,and weakening does not end flooding or electrical hazards.','₹4,600crore is not a settled Murasu loss figure simply because it appears in a report about the approaching second storm.','How should disaster reporting communicate confirmed events and evolving impacts without confusing the public?','APLOSS,APREL,APLETTER',3,ap=True)
add('Andhra Pradesh','02 · Murasu chronology and accountable recovery','Immediate relief: ₹37 crore district allocation is separate from central assistance','The2NovemberIANSreport describes immediate AP relief funding to17districts as Murasu approached. Original treasury orders and individual expenditures were not inspected.','The breakdown is six districts×₹3crore,eight×₹2crore and three×₹1crore:₹18+₹16+₹3=₹37crore.|Reported uses include evacuation,food,shelters,medicine,fuel,transport and essential-service restoration,with emergency treasury permission referenced.|A state release,a district authorisation,actual expenditure,beneficiary receipt and a later central-assistance request are not interchangeable.','Emergency spending should be rapid but retain lawful purpose,records,procurement integrity and later reconciliation. Disaster relief is not automatically insurance compensation for every private loss.','Collectors need operational flexibility alongside clear responsibilities,accessible records and verification that support reaches vulnerable households. Do not infer equal district amounts or complete adequacy from the aggregate.|For APPSC,distinguish this state emergency envelope from regular tax devolution and from the CM’s subsequent request to the Union. The latter report’s15-day detailed-assessment plan is not proof the report or central payment already occurred by7November.','₹37crore is neither₹37crore per district nor independently verified central assistance sanctioned in response to the later letter.','How can emergency relief combine speed,equity and expenditure accountability?','APREL,APLETTER',2,ap=True)
add('Andhra Pradesh','03 · Lifeline restoration and livelihood evidence','Murasu lifelines: restoration percentages need a timestamp and the right denominator','TNIE/PTI’s6Novemberreport gives a5Novemberreview snapshot for power,telecom,roads and drainage. It does not certify complete statewide service recovery.','APCPDCL had reportedly restored871of1,103affected11kVfeeders and repaired1,493of3,946damaged poles;feeder and pole counts measure different tasks.|30,518of31,428telecom towers were reported operational,about97%;this does not prove97%ofallusers had reliable service at every location.|Road damage,cleared trees/drain blockages and ongoing flood monitoring show interacting infrastructure risks;original engineering audits were not inspected.','An energised feeder,an operational tower,household connection,usable road and safe restored service are distinct outcomes. Network totals and affected-asset totals have different denominators.','Prioritise hospitals,water,shelters and communications with safe isolation,testing and documented handover. A rapid return of electricity can still be unsafe if flood-damaged consumer installations are not checked.|For APPSC,compare restoration inputs with continuity,reach and quality. Cross-state support and official progress reports are valuable,but an announcement or high percentage cannot replace verification of unresolved local pockets and public safety.','97%oftowers operational is not automatically97%ofhouseholds connected,complete repair or final audited recovery.','How should post-disaster lifeline restoration be measured and governed?','APLOSS',6,ap=True,report=True)
add('Andhra Pradesh','03 · Lifeline restoration and livelihood evidence','Crop-loss assessment: do not merge differing post-Murasu estimates','Two6Novemberreports describe preliminary livelihood losses,but their horticulture figures differ. Keep the source,date,sector and assessment stage attached rather than combining them into one invented series.','IANS’sletter account gives agriculture1,43,122hectares/1,86,063farmers and horticulture6,504.38hectares/12,178farmers.|TNIE/PTI’sreview snapshot gives rounded agriculture1.4lakhhectares/over1.8lakhfarmers and horticulture6,749hectares/12,586farmers.|The IANS account estimates agriculture₹874.96crore and horticulture₹337.10crore;original survey/letter and final totals were not inspected.','Affected area,fully lost output,farmer count,tenancy,insured area and compensation eligibility are distinct. Water receding may change an initial assessment.','Use ground verification,geotagged evidence where appropriate,accessible corrections and explicit treatment of tenants,fishers and vulnerable workers. Digitised claims still need fair appeals and safeguards against exclusion.|For APPSC,connect crop rehabilitation,seed/input support,market access,drainage and aquaculture without claiming a universal compensation rate or completed payout. A sector estimate is not automatically the farmer’s individual entitlement or an audited statewide account.','The differing horticulture counts cannot be silently averaged,added or described as a verified revision without evidence.','How can disaster-loss assessment support timely and equitable livelihood recovery?','APLETTER,APLOSS',6,ap=True,report=True)
assert len(J)==24
# Typography, including numeric boundaries, while preserving scientific acronyms.
import re
fix={'114parcels':'114 parcels','264parcels':'264 parcels','12October':'12 October','6November':'6 November','2November':'2 November','3November':'3 November','5November':'5 November','7November':'7 November','IANS’sletter':'IANS’s letter','TNIE/PTI’sreview':'TNIE/PTI’s review','hectares/':' hectares / ','ofallusers':'of all users','oftowers':'of towers','ofhouseholds':'of households','allusers':'all users','kVfeeders':'kV feeders'}
def clean(s):
 for a,b in fix.items():s=s.replace(a,b)
 for x in ['crore','districts','farmers','hectares','parcels','lakh','affected','damaged','telecom','of','and']:
  s=re.sub(r'(?<=\d)'+x+r'\b',' '+x,s)
 s=re.sub(r'(?<=[,;:])(?=[A-Za-z₹])',' ',s);s=re.sub(r'(?<=[A-Za-z])(?=₹)',' ',s);s=re.sub(r'(?<=%)(?=[A-Za-z])',' ',s)
 return s
for n in J:
 for k in ['block','title','why','static_link','trap','mains']:n[k]=clean(n[k])
 for k in ['facts','analysis']:n[k]=[clean(x)for x in n[k]]
N.extend(J)
for p,x in [('notes.json',N),('sources.json',S),('source-access.json',A)]:save('data/'+p,x)
print('24 new notes plus expanded CA-043;',len(N),'total notes;',len(S),'references')
